      <a href="#lactose-casein">Lactose and Casein</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste and Mixing</a>
      <a href="#vs-competitors">Designer Whey vs Rivals</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Designer Whey is a light, stevia-sweetened whey blend with 20 g of protein and 110 calories per scoop, but the current formula is not pure whey: its protein blend lists whey concentrate, micellar casein and whey isolate, per the brand. It is also not third-party certified as far as we could find. It is still a reasonable supermarket whey at $1.91 per 25 g in the 4 lb tub. This review covers the label, testing, lactose, the rest of the lineup and the cost per gram.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Designer Whey Protein Powder Review: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Designer Whey is a decent everyday whey for people who want a lighter, low-sugar shake with no artificial sweeteners, and its 4 lb tub is fairly priced at $1.91 per 25 g of protein. It is not a pure whey isolate, it is not third-party certified, and 20 g per scoop is on the low side for a 31 g serving. Think of it as a mild supermarket blend with probiotics and fiber, not a performance isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy it if you want a stevia-only whey that is easy to find at grocery stores, you like that it adds prebiotic fiber and probiotics, or you want an unsweetened unflavored version for cooking. Skip it if you are lactose intolerant enough to need an isolate, if you are drug-tested in sport, or if you want 25 g or more of protein per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            One point of confusion to clear up first: Designer Whey is not Designs for Sport. Designs for Sport is the sport line of Designs for Health, a separate company that makes an NSF-certified beef protein, which we cover in our <a href="/designs-for-sport-beef-protein-review/">Designs for Sport beef protein review</a>. Designer Whey comes from Designer Wellness, sold for years under the Designer Protein name, and that name still appears on older tubs and many retailer listings.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Designer Whey, per 31 g scoop (label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per scoop</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">110</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Added sugar</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.91</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g, 4 lb tub</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Designer Wellness Protein Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Designer Wellness sells several powders under the Designer name, and only one is the classic Designer Whey. The five below are the protein powders in the current lineup, ranked by how useful they are to a typical buyer. Prices are from the brand site on 21 November 2026 and nutrition figures are from the brand's product pages at the time of writing; both vary by flavor and retailer.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Designer Whey Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not as far as the brand states or any public database shows. We searched the NSF Certified for Sport product list, the Informed Sport search and the Clean Label Project site on 21 November 2026 and found no Designer Whey or Designer Wellness product in any of them. The Designer Whey product pages list gluten free, kosher certified, rBGH-free and non-GMO whey, and made in a peanut-free facility in the USA, but they do not mention independent lab testing or a verifying body.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gluten free and kosher are real certifications, but neither checks for banned substances, label accuracy or heavy metals. The brand does not publish a certificate of analysis or contaminant results on its product pages, so the honest answer to whether Designer Whey is tested for heavy metals is that the brand does not say. Whey proteins generally test lower for lead and cadmium than plant or cocoa-heavy powders, but that is a category tendency, not a result for this product.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most gym-goers and casual users, the absence of a sport certification is a minor point. For athletes in tested sport it is the deciding one: a powder without lot-level certification carries a small but real risk of contamination with a banned substance, and "made in the USA" does not change that. If that applies to you, pick from our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/">NSF-certified protein powder list</a> instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you still prefer Designer Whey and want reassurance, contact Designer Wellness and ask for a recent certificate of analysis for your flavor and lot. A brand that tests will usually share protein assay, microbial and heavy-metal results on request; a vague answer tells you something too.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TESTING ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Kosher and gluten free, yes. Third-party sport or purity certification, no.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">At the time of writing, no Designer Whey product appears in the NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Clean Label Project listings we searched, and the brand pages do not mention independent testing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Designer Whey Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Designer Whey lists 20 g of protein and 110 calories in a 31 g scoop, per the brand's French Vanilla and Gourmet Chocolate pages at the time of writing. Each scoop has 6 to 7 g of carbohydrate, of which 3 to 4 g is fiber and 2 g is naturally occurring sugar, with 0 g of added sugar, 1.5 to 2 g of fat, 40 mg of cholesterol and about 100 mg of sodium. That makes it about 65 percent protein by weight, lower than most whey isolates, because fiber and the digestive blend take up part of the scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein comes from what the brand calls the Designer Protein Blend: rBGH-free whey protein concentrate, rBGH-free micellar casein and rBGH-free whey protein isolate, in that order. Ingredients are listed by weight, so concentrate is the largest part and isolate the smallest. The name Designer Whey and the "100% whey" wording on some older tubs and retailer listings do not match this current label, which includes casein, a different milk protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            A Digestive Health Blend follows: GMO-free prebiotic vegetable fiber, three probiotic strains (Lactobacillus acidophilus, Bifidobacterium longum and L. plantarum) and vegetarian protease enzymes. The brand does not list a probiotic count in CFU on the pages we checked, so it is not possible to compare the dose with a dedicated probiotic. If gut support is a priority, our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-probiotics/">protein powder with probiotics</a> compares the brands that do state CFU counts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest is short: alkalized cocoa in the chocolate flavors, then under 2 percent each of natural flavors, sea salt, xanthan gum, stevia extract and sunflower lecithin. There is no sucralose, acesulfame potassium or erythritol, per the brand. The Unflavored version drops the flavorings and stevia altogether, with a 28 g scoop that lists 100 calories and the same 20 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labels have changed over the years, which is why retailer listings disagree. Some grocery listings still show 18 or 19 g of protein and 100 calories per scoop, and an older label in the NIH Dietary Supplement Label Database lists a 26 g scoop with 2 g of sugar. Check the tub in front of you rather than the listing photo, especially if you are buying from a third-party seller.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lactose-casein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Does Designer Whey Contain Lactose or Casein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes to both. Designer Whey contains milk, the current blend includes micellar casein, and the brand describes the powder as 95 percent lactose free rather than lactose free. The 2 g of naturally occurring sugar per scoop is mostly lactose from the whey concentrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            For many people with mild lactose intolerance, 2 g of lactose is tolerable; a cup of milk has about 12 g. The brand itself suggests that dairy-sensitive users may still notice effects and should start with half a scoop. If you are more sensitive than that, a whey isolate or a lactose-free powder is the safer route, and our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder guide</a> lists the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The casein is not a flaw in itself. Micellar casein digests more slowly than whey, which can make a shake feel more filling and is why some brands blend the two. But if you specifically want a fast-digesting whey for after training, or you avoid casein because of a casein sensitivity, Designer Whey is the wrong product. Our explainer on <a href="/casein-vs-whey-protein-powder/">casein vs whey protein</a> covers when each makes sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need no dairy at all, Designer Wellness also sells Designer Egg, made from egg white and egg yolk protein, which the brand lists as dairy free and lactose free with 24 g of protein per 32 g serving. A milk allergy, as distinct from lactose intolerance, rules out every Designer Whey product, and anyone with a diagnosed allergy should check labels with a doctor or dietitian.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-25g" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does Designer Whey Cost per 25 g of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Designer Whey costs $1.91 per 25 g of protein in the 4 lb tub and $2.59 per 25 g in the 2 lb tub, at the brand-site prices we saw on 21 November 2026. The 4 lb French Vanilla tub lists 59 servings of 20 g for $89.99; the 2 lb Gourmet Chocolate lists 29 servings for $59.99. Grocery and online retailers often sell it for less, so check the shelf price against these figures.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Designer product and size</th>
